internationalConferencesCard6DetailBody:
Termez University of Economics and Service, in cooperation with the Ministry of Higher Education, Science and Innovation, hosted an international scientific and practical conference titled “Current issues in linguistics and literary studies.”

The conference aimed to promote research in linguistics and literary studies, explore international experience, and strengthen academic cooperation.

Scholars, professors, lecturers, and young researchers from Uzbekistan, Russia, Belarus, Tajikistan, and other countries took part, including:

Igor Leonovich Kopylov — Director of the Yakub Kolas Institute of Linguistics, Center for Belarusian Culture, Language and Literature Studies, National Academy of Sciences of Belarus;

Ivan Vasilievich Saverchenko — Director of the Yanka Kupala Institute of Literary Studies;

Kristina Grigoryevna Golubeva — Director at the N.A. Dobrolyubov Nizhny Novgorod State Linguistic University;

Poyon Uralovich Bakirov — Doctor of Philological Sciences, Professor at Termez State University;

Irina Vladimirovna Suljina — Coordinator of Educational Programs at the Representative Office of Rossotrudnichestvo under the Embassy of the Russian Federation;

Eraj Begijonovich Boyev — Candidate of Historical Sciences, Director of the Scientific and Educational Center for Oriental Studies, N.A. Dobrolyubov Nizhny Novgorod State Linguistic University;

Irina Vladimirovna Yelintseva — Candidate of Philological Sciences, Head of the Department of Belarusian–Russian Linguistic Relations, Yakub Kolas Institute of Linguistics;

Galina Borisovna Yesenova — Candidate of Philological Sciences, Leading Specialist of the Scientific Department, B.B. Gorodovikov Kalmyk State University;

Khusraw Jamshidovich Shambezoda — Doctor of Philological Sciences, Professor at the Department of Theoretical and Applied Linguistics, Russian–Tajik (Slavonic) University;

Azim Sodiqovich Aminov — Candidate of Philological Sciences, Associate Professor at the Russian–Tajik (Slavonic) University.

Professors and lecturers from Kokand State University and Yodju Technical Institute also presented their research.

The forum offered a platform for scholarly exchange, new research directions, and stronger cooperation across countries.

Reports were presented in areas including:

The Modern Russian Language: Current State and Development Trends;

Issues of Comparative Studies between Russian and Other Languages;

Theoretical and Practical Aspects of Teaching Russian in Schools and Universities;

Topical Issues in Communicative Linguistics;

Translation Theory: Problems and Solutions;

Theory and History of Literary Studies;

Interliterary Relations among Nations;

World Classical Literature and Its Contemporary Interpretations.

Participants exchanged experience, discussed joint research projects, and explored opportunities for future publications and collaboration.
